import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={keplerLaws:{theory:{concept:"[[Kepler's three laws describe how planets, comets and asteroids move around the Sun.]] First law: every planet moves along an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. Second law: the line joining the planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times, so a planet moves fastest when it is closest to the Sun. Third law: the square of the orbital period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of the orbit \u2014 in years and astronomical units, T\xB2 = a\xB3."},section:{history:{title:"How the laws were found",text:"For centuries people believed that planets move in perfect circles. The Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe measured the positions of the planets with unmatched accuracy, and his assistant Johannes Kepler spent years trying to fit the orbit of Mars with circles. It would not fit \u2014 by a tiny amount, but Kepler trusted the measurements. In 1609 he published the first two laws and in 1619 the third."},first:{title:"1st law: the orbit is an ellipse",text:"[[An ellipse is the shape a gardener draws with a loop of string around two pins: the two distances from any point of the line to the two foci always add up to the same length, r\u2081 + r\u2082 = 2a, where a is the semi-major axis (half of the longest width).]] In the picture the Sun is at one focus, the empty focus is marked with a cross, and the two lines to the planet always add up to 2a."},eccentricity:{title:"How stretched is the ellipse?",text:"The eccentricity e tells how stretched the ellipse is: e = 0 is a circle (both foci in the same place), and e close to 1 is a very long thin ellipse. The planets' orbits are almost circles \u2014 Earth's e is 0.017, Mars's 0.093, Mercury's 0.206. Comets are different: Halley's comet has e = 0.967, so it comes within 0.59 AU of the Sun and then flies out beyond Neptune. The nearest point of the orbit is a(1 \u2212 e), called perihelion; the farthest is a(1 + e), called aphelion."},second:{title:"2nd law: equal areas in equal times",text:"The picture divides the orbit into 12 slices of equal time: near the Sun the slices are short and wide, far from the Sun they are long and narrow \u2014 but all have the same area. [[So the planet must move fastest at perihelion and slowest at aphelion: the speeds are in the ratio (1 + e)/(1 \u2212 e).]] Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, and it does move a little faster then (30.3 km/s against 29.3 km/s in July)."},third:{title:"3rd law: the year and the orbit",text:"Mars has a = 1.524 AU, so T\xB2 = 3.54 and T = 1.88 years. Jupiter: a = 5.2 AU gives T = 11.9 years. [[The law holds for any shape of ellipse: an orbit with e = 0.9 and a = 1 AU also takes exactly one year, like Earth's.]]"},newton:{title:"Why do the laws hold?",text:"Kepler only described the motion; Newton explained it. The Sun's gravity pulls with a force that falls as 1/r\xB2, and that single fact produces ellipses (1st law). The pull always points to the Sun, so it cannot change the planet's angular momentum \u2014 and constant angular momentum is exactly the 2nd law. The 3rd law follows from the same 1/r\xB2 force. The same laws describe the Moon around the Earth and every satellite \u2014 see the Orbits simulation."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A planet has a = 4 AU. How long is its year? (T = 4^1.5 = 8 years.) Where does a comet move fastest? (Closest to the Sun, at perihelion.) Can the Sun be at the centre of an orbit? (Only if e = 0, a circle; otherwise it is at a focus.) Why do the two lines r\u2081 and r\u2082 always add up to the same length? (That is the definition of the ellipse.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{first:"1st law: the distances to the two foci add up to the length of the long axis",second:"2nd law: the swept area grows evenly with time",third:"3rd law: T in years, a in astronomical units",extremes:"Nearest (perihelion) and farthest (aphelion) distances",minor:"Semi-minor axis (half of the shortest width)",arealSpeed:"Area swept per unit of time: the area of the whole ellipse divided by the period",visViva:"Speed at the distance r from the Sun (GM: the Sun's gravitational parameter)",speedRatio:"Ratio of the greatest speed (perihelion) to the smallest (aphelion)"},variable:{a:"Semi-major axis",e:"Eccentricity (0 is a circle)",r:"Distance from the Sun",T:"Orbital period",v:"Orbital speed"},assumption:{onlySun:"Only the Sun's gravity acts; the planets' pull on each other and the Sun's own motion are ignored, so the orbit is an exact closed ellipse.",flat:"The orbit is drawn flat, as seen from above; real orbits are tilted slightly against each other.",scale:"Not to scale: the Sun and the planet are drawn far larger than their real size."},param:{body:"Body",e:"Eccentricity (stretch)",a:"Semi-major axis",pace:"Speed (orbits per second)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{body:'Pick a real planet or comet, or choose "Your own orbit" to change its shape yourself.',e:"0 is a circle; close to 1 is a long, thin ellipse.",a:"The size of the orbit: half of its longest width, in astronomical units (AU).",pace:"How fast the animation runs, in orbits per second; the real period is shown below the picture."},preset:{circle:"Circle",earthLike:"Earth",oval:"Oval",cometLike:"Comet",mercuryLike:"Mercury",jupiterLike:"Jupiter",neptuneLike:"Neptune"},body:{custom:"Your own orbit",mercury:"Mercury",venus:"Venus",earth:"Earth",mars:"Mars",jupiter:"Jupiter",saturn:"Saturn",uranus:"Uranus",neptune:"Neptune",halley:"Halley's comet"},measure:{r1:"Distance from the Sun",r2:"Distance from the empty focus",sumR:"Sum of the two distances",period:"Orbital period",years:"Time since perihelion",arealSpeed:"Area swept per year (AU\xB2 / yr)",sectorArea:"Area of one sector of equal time (AU\xB2)",periodSquared:"Period squared (yr\xB2)",semiMajorCubed:"Semi-major axis cubed (AU\xB3)",keplerRatio:"Ratio T\xB2 / a\xB3 (yr\xB2 / AU\xB3)",perihelion:"Perihelion (nearest point)",aphelion:"Aphelion (farthest point)",speedRatio:"Fastest speed / slowest speed",sweptArea:"Total area swept (AU\xB2)"},view:{planet:"Planet",sun:"Sun",emptyFocus:"empty focus",earthOrbit:"Earth's orbit, 1 AU",area:"Equal times ({time}), equal areas: {area} AU\xB2 each",sum:"r\u2081 + r\u2082 = {r1} + {r2} = {sum} (that is 2a)",time:"Period T = {period} \xB7 elapsed {elapsed} \xB7 T\xB2 = {t2} \xB7 a\xB3 = {a3}",fociOption:"Law 1: the two foci and distances",areasOption:"Law 2: equal areas in equal times",referenceOption:"Law 3: Earth's orbit for comparison"},graph:{distances:"Distances from the two foci (their sum stays constant)",speed:"Speed against time",area:"Total area swept against time (a straight line)"},challenge:{circle:{title:"A perfect circle",description:"Make an orbit that is a circle: the two foci in the same place.",hint:'Choose "Your own orbit" and set the eccentricity to 0.'},comet:{title:"A comet's orbit",description:"Make a very long, thin ellipse like a comet's, with eccentricity 0.9 or more.",hint:"Raise the eccentricity, or pick Halley's comet."},yearEight:{title:"A year of eight years",description:"Make a planet whose year lasts 8 Earth years.",hint:"T\xB2 = a\xB3, so a = T^(2/3): for 8 years, a = 4 AU."},speedRatio:{title:"Racing past the Sun",description:"Make the planet at perihelion move at least 5 times faster than at aphelion (but keep the eccentricity below 0.9).",hint:"The speeds are in the ratio (1 + e)/(1 \u2212 e); you need e of about 0.67 or more."},earthYear:{title:"Earth's year, a comet's orbit",description:"Make an orbit with eccentricity at least 0.7 whose period is one year, like Earth's.",hint:"The period depends only on a, not on the shape: keep a = 1 AU and stretch the ellipse."},sungrazer:{title:"A sungrazer",description:"Make an orbit that passes closer than 0.05 AU to the Sun, and has a period of at least 5 years.",hint:"The nearest distance is a(1 \u2212 e): use a large a and an eccentricity near 0.99."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
